% c262 (A&S Unit 27, PDF 469-470 sec. 7, Ex. 27-10): 162 beat 2 Tpt 1 F4 (was G4 under the violas' and horns' A flat4) and Hn 4 G3 (was F3), so the off-beat chord keeps the root G a minor ninth under the 9th, as Hn 3-4 and Bn 1 do at 22.
\version "2.24.0"
% c258 (expression; Sebesky ch. 1 Ex. 2A, PDF 17-18: each layer of an orchestration carries its own word for the sound wanted, "(EASY)" on the harp's figure; PDF 13-14 focus): the broken-chord eighth accompaniment of F1 is marked "leggiero" at each entry (11 Cl 1-2, 19 violas, 83 violas, 151 Cl 1-2), so it stays under the tune that shares its register. Dynamics unchanged.
% c126: Cl 2 breath at the end of 170 (its pad ran 167-182 without a rest).
% RECAPITULATION, bars 151-170: theme F1 ff, fuller than at 11 (flutes and oboes on the tune from the start, trumpets and horns 3-4
% off the beat, trombones holding the harmony, tuba on the bass of the consequent). 166 closes on i6 (c133; 26 is deceptive).
% c043 (form; A&S Unit 19, PDF 306, 316-317, 319-320): 151-152 the bass stays on the retransition's G under F1's head (accented, expanded cadential 6/4:
% i6/4 151, iv over the G pedal 152, V 153, V6-V4/3 154), so the thematic return (151) comes before the tonic root (155, timpani C); 11-12 have E flat-C | D-F.
% c133 (form; A&S Unit 16, PDF 249-250: descending fifths are goal-directed, VI-II-V a segment of the chain; PDF 250: the chord that ends a motion carries the weight): 166 ends F1 on the tonic, V-i6 (bass G-E flat; the exposition's deceptive cadence at 26 opened P2, which the recapitulation omits), so the only deceptive cadence onto A flat in the recapitulation is 186, which opens the A flat major statement (187).
% 167-170: link, VI - iiø6/5 - V6/5 (descending fifths after the i6 of 166), diminuendo to p: the second theme in C minor follows from the inverted V (no root V-i in C).
% c242 (A&S Unit 29, PDF 512-513: from VI add sharp 4, augmented sixths often follow VI; PDF 514 Ex. 29-4b: II4/3 with flat 6 in the bass and sharp 4 is the French 4/3; PDF 517: the French sixth's 2 removes the fifths; PDF 503: augmented sixths mark the end of a phase): 168 is a French sixth A flat-C-D-F sharp over the A flat held from 167 (Vc, Cb, Bn 2), Bn 1 E flat3-F sharp3-G3-F3, Hn 2 and Cl 2 D4, violins F sharp-G-A flat-F sharp-D; 169 V7 flat 9 over G (bass flat 6-5, F sharp-G), 170 V6/5 as before, so the link reaches the second theme from an inverted V (no root V-i in C) and 169 no longer sounds the stop chord over B. Vc/Cb 169-170 slurred in one up-bow (c218).

% c062 (harmony; A&S Unit 28, PDF 482-485, 495 sec. 16): 163 beat 3 the recapitulation sharpens F1's consequent: D becomes D flat over F (apparent Neapolitan 6/5, F-A flat-C-D flat), so the line falls E flat-D flat-C-B (the diminished 3rd filled in) and the D natural returns only as a tone of V in 164 (Ex. 28-6); Tpt 2 and Tbn 2 take F. The exposition (23) keeps the diatonic ii.
