% c107 (texture; Adler ch. 5, PDF 136: a change of texture for the consequent; PDF 139: 'melodizing' the second violins sets off the second statement; PDF 142-144: a middleground countertheme in its own rhythm): 159-165 Vln II leaves the tune (still in Fl, Ob, Vln I, Vla, Hn 1-2) and plays a countersubject in slurred eighths after P2's (27-34), fitted to the consequent's harmony (no B against the suspended C at 165 beats 3-4).
% c108 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 133: an ornamental line moves independently of the melody; PDF 129-130: marks that stress a part): 159 Vln II restate ff with 'espress.' at the start of the counter-line.
% c167 (texture; Sebesky ch. 3, PDF 138 rule 2: the lead line is carried by more players than any subordinate line; PDF 164: a wind doubling a string line is marked one level softer): since c109 each octave of F1's consequent has half of Vln I (C5 strand: Vln I lower half + Ob 1), while the counter-line in the same octave has all of Vln II ff plus Cl 1 (c166). 159 Vln II f espress. (was ff), Cl 1-2 mf (were f; equal on the shared staff), Ob 2's held notes mf; the tune stays ff. c108's purpose (mark the start of the counter-line) is kept by 'espress.' and by the clarinet doubling, which c108 did not have.
recPVlnTwo = { c''2->(\ff g'4. aes'8 | bes'4-.) aes'8( g'8 f'4-.) es'4-. | d'4( g'8 aes'8 b'4 d''4) | f''2-> es''4( d''4) | es''2->( bes'4. c''8 | des''4-.) c''8( bes'8 aes'4-.) g'4-. | f'8( aes'8 c''8 f''8 aes''2) | g''2.-> r4 | es''8(\f^\markup \italic "espress." d'' c'' b') c''( es'' g'' es'') | bes'8( d'' f'' d'') aes'( c'' f'' d'') | b'8( g' b' d'') f''( d'' b' d'') | f''8( d'' b' g') d''( b' g' b') | es''8( c'' g' c'') f'( aes' des'' aes') | d''8( b' g' b') d''( f'' d'' b') | b'8( d'' g'' d'') g'( d' g' d') | c'2->\ff r2 | r4 es''8(\f d'' c'' bes') aes'( g' | fis'8 g' aes' fis' d'4) r4 | r4 f'8(\> es' d' c') b( aes | g1)\p | }
% c202 (A&S Unit 12, PDF 177: the 7th of II7 enters as a common tone and resolves down by step; PDF 191 point 2): 168 is ii-half-dim7 in root position (D-F-A flat-C) with its 7th C4 in the violas alone, held from 167; it rose to D4 at 169. Violas now resolve C4 to B3 at 169 and take D4 at 170, where Hn 2 leaves its D4.
% c214 (Adler ch. 15, PDF 156: the accompaniment begins first, so the melody's entrance sounds fresh (Weber, Ex. XV-7); an anacrusis before a colour helps it be perceived (Tchaikovsky, Ex. XV-6)):
% 170 the violas hold D4 for two beats, then start their sul tasto pp eighths on V6/5 (D3-F3, the seventh F falling to E flat3 at 171), so the cor anglais and muted trumpet enter over a figure already running.
recPVla = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f8( aes8 c'8 f'8 aes'2) | g'2.-> r4 | c'2->( g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( f8 aes8 b4 d'4) | aes'2-> g'4( f'4) | es'2->( des'4. c'8 | b4-.) c'8( d'8 g4-.) f4-. | es'4( d'8 c'8~ c'4 b4) | c'2-> r2 | c'1\f | c'1\> | b1 | d'2\p \tag #'score { \once \override TextScript.self-alignment-X = #CENTER } d8(\pp^\markup \italic "sul tasto" f d f) | }
% c218 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 54: going from arco to pizzicato is easiest when the last stroke before it is an up-bow, so the hand arrives at the frog; PDF 38: a diminuendo lies naturally on a down-bow): 167 Vc and Cb take A flat up-bow after the half rest, so 168's diminuendo is a down-bow and the held B (169-170) an up-bow that ends at the frog, a quarter rest (0.36 s at 2 = 84) before the pizz. at 171; by plain alternation the B was a down-bow ending at the tip. Same as 114 (c175 up-bow before the pizz. at 115).
recPVc = { g,2\ff g,2 | g,2 g,2 | g,4 g4 f4 d4 | b,2 d2 | c2 g,2 | f2 d2 | f,2 d2 | g,2 g,4 r4 | es2 c2 | d2 f,2 | g,4 g4 f4 d4 | b,2 d2 | c2 f2 | g4 f4 es4 d4 | g,2 g,2 | es,2-> r2 | aes,1\f\upbow | aes,1\> | g,1( | b,2.)\p r4 | }
% c246 (Adler App. A, adler2 PDF 302; adler1 PDF 108): 166 E flat1 with the C extension, an octave below the cellos and with the tuba and contrabassoon, so the deceptive cadence's E flat is not the one bass note the strings fold up.
recPCb = { g,,2\ff g,,2 | g,,2 g,,2 | g,,2 r4 g,,4 | b,,2 d,2 | c,2 g,,2 | f,2 d,2 | f,,2 d,2 | g,,2 g,,4 r4 | es,2 c,2 | d,2 f,,2 | g,,2 r4 g,,4 | b,,2 d,2 | c,2 f,2 | g,2 g,,2 | g,,2 g,,2 | es,,2-> r2 | aes,,1\f\upbow | aes,,1\> | g,,1( | b,,2.)\p r4 | }
% c075 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues in long rests, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part (score.ly removes it from the score); Vln II 167-170 (the falling line, an octave below Vln I) before the cor anglais solo at 171, after 20 bars
recPCorAnglais = { R1*16 | \tag #'score { R1*4 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnTwo" #UP { R1*4^\markup \tiny "Vln. II" } } | }
